NOT a backport — this fix does not exist upstream yet. Verified still present on
`edge` at eff086c1 (332 commits past v0.9-FINAL), where the same function has moved
to src/preloaded/vm/dev_lua.erl and snapshot/3 is byte-identical; `luerl:gc` appears
nowhere in the edge tree. A PR is drafted in anyone-protocol
docs/hyperbeam-migration/luerl-gc-pr-draft.md; drop this patch once it lands.

dev_lua:snapshot/3 serialises the whole luerl VM with
term_to_binary(luerl:externalize(State)) and never garbage-collects it. Luerl frees
table-store slots only inside luerl_heap:gc/1, and nothing in HyperBEAM ever calls
it, so the snapshot retains every table the process has ever allocated and grows
without bound even when the Lua-visible state is a fixed size. The blob is written
twice per slot, which made per-message cost grow with accumulated slots.

Note luerl:externalize/1 is misleadingly named: it is luerl_lib_math:externalize/1
and converts only the RNG state so it survives term_to_binary. It does not touch the
table store.

Measured on this image at v0.9-FINAL, a token contract with a fixed-size balance map:

                            stock          patched
  VM blob @ slot 2          400,273 B      176,346 B
  VM blob @ slot 150     20,600,117 B      176,346 B  (byte-identical, same hash)
  store bytes / msg @ 150 41,331,145 B      483,603 B  (~85x)
  latency slot 2 -> 150     537 -> 905 ms   579 -> 544 ms (flat)

Collecting here is contained: snapshot/3 does not write the collected state back into
`priv`, so it garbage-collects a copy purely for serialisation and the running VM
continues on the uncollected state. The entire behavioural surface is restore.

Validated against our suites, patched vs stock: Tier-1 298/0, Tier-2 205/0, D10 E2E
21/21, adversarial restore-fidelity identical to stock, real-contract state
byte-identical across a node restart, and a sustained 10-round workload (39/0, 40/0)
where per-round latency is 1.01x across ten rounds patched versus 2.88x stock.

DO NOT "simplify" this by calling collectgarbage() from Lua instead. luerl_heap:gc/1
derives its root set from the state handed to it, so it is only sound at a quiescent
point like this one. Called from inside a running Lua program it cannot see the
in-flight continuation: any pcall on the dynamic stack, or an in-flight ipairs/pairs
over a temporary table, leaves live objects unreachable from the root set and they
are freed. The result is a raw Erlang {badkey,N} from luerl_heap:get_env_var/3 or
luerl_lib_basic:ipairs_next/3, raised outside Lua where no pcall can catch it.
Measured on luerl 1.3.0, the version this VERSION vendors.

